// vygenerováno scripts/gen-weekly.mjs, needitovat ručně
export const WEEKLY: { subject: string; preheader: string; body: string }[] = [
  { subject: "How to order when you eat out", preheader: "Protein first, then vegetables, sauce on the side.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nThe menu is open and the waiter is already looking at you.\n\nBuild the plate the same way as at home. About a quarter is protein: meat, fish, eggs, beans or lentils. About a quarter is a quality carb: potatoes, rice, or a wholegrain side. About half is vegetables. Fat is seasoning: a little oil or sauce. Drink water, unsweetened tea, or coffee without the sweet extras.\n\nRead the menu in that order. Protein first. Vegetables next. One carb portion. Sauce on the side. Fat packs a lot of calories into a small volume, and a kitchen pouring by eye adds them fast.\n\nYour total calories over the day and the week decide fat loss. Fit the meal you want into that total. Sweet drinks, juice and alcohol add calories. Liquid calories don't fill you up the way food does. Alcohol often leads to more food on top.\n\nSweets can stay too. Fit them into the day. A lifelong ban is hard to keep, and that's often when people drop the whole diet.\n\nHow to build the plate, step by step: {{link_article:what-to-eat-to-lose-fat}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "Portions when there's no scale around", preheader: "Fourteen honest days. Then only the foods that fool the eye.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nYou don't have to weigh every meal for the rest of the year.\n\nCounting is there to train your eye. For fourteen days, log everything. Cooking fat. Drinks. Bites while you cook. The things you tell yourself don't count. With my clients, very often the deficit was never there. Those bits just didn't make it into the log.\n\nFourteen days teaches you the basics. After two to three months of checking now and then, most of my clients estimate normal meals well enough. They go back to the scale only for the foods they keep getting wrong. Those are usually oil, nuts and sauces. A piece of meat or fish is easy to judge. Fats are where the eye guesses low.\n\nThere's a well-known 1992 study on this blind spot. People who said they ate under 1,200 kcal a day and still weren't losing weight had their energy use measured. Low energy use was ruled out, so their metabolism wasn't the problem. They underreported intake by 47% on average, and they overreported activity by 51%. That's simply what estimating does, and it happens to all of us.\n\nSo the scale is your teacher for the first fourteen days. After that, weigh the foods that still fool you, and estimate the rest.\n\nCheck your calorie target again here: {{link_kit}}\nThe full article: {{link_article:calorie-deficit-how-much-to-eat}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "Written coaching, if you want it", preheader: "You send a 3-minute report each week, and I send a written plan back.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nKeep about 80 to 90% of your calories as a solid base, and leave 10 to 20% for food you eat just because you enjoy it. One dessert is part of the plan. A party, a restaurant, a visit: they fit if the day still hits calories and protein.\n\nPlenty of people manage this on their own. If you want me to set the week with you, this is the written version for English-speaking clients.\n\nYou apply in writing. I usually reply on working days, with a few questions. After payment you fill in an intake. After your intake, you get your calories, protein and macros, a meal plan built around the way you already eat (restaurants included) and a training plan for your real schedule.\n\nThen once a week you send a 3-minute report from your phone: weight, waist, food, steps, how the week went. I answer in writing with the plan for the next week and I recalculate the numbers.\n\nNo calls and no video calls. You get every plan and every number in writing, and you can read it again on the plane.\n\n{{price_block}}\n\nApply here: {{link_apply}}\n\nWant more support? Message me for VIP options.\n\nOr reply to this email with a few lines: age, job, how a normal week looks, what you already tried. I'll tell you honestly if it makes sense.\n\nIf you're pregnant or breastfeeding, have an eating disorder, take medication that affects weight, or have a medical condition, talk to your doctor first. I'm a coach, not a doctor. This is also not for anyone under 18.\n\nRead the 80/20 article here: {{link_article:flexible-dieting}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "Protein: 80 kg x 1.6 = 128 g", preheader: "1.6 to 2.2 g per kg. One protein food in every meal.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nAfter total calories, the next thing I'd check in your food is protein.\n\nFor most active people, 1.6 to 2.2 g per kg of body weight a day works well. At 80 kg:\n\n80 x 1.6 = 128 g\n80 x 2.2 = 176 g\n\nThat's about 130 to 175 g a day. If you carry a lot of extra weight, use your goal weight instead of your current weight. If you're losing fat, aim at the higher end. It helps you keep muscle and keeps hunger down.\n\nA 2018 meta-analysis of strength training studies found that the benefit of extra protein for muscle flattens out at about 1.6 g per kg a day. I use 1.6 to 2.2 g to leave some margin. A 2012 meta-analysis of diet trials found that higher-protein diets gave a bit more fat loss and kept more lean mass than standard-protein diets.\n\nYou don't have to weigh every gram to get close. Put a protein food on the plate at breakfast, lunch and dinner.\n\nMeat and fish. Eggs. Greek yogurt, skyr, quark (a fresh cheese, similar to strained yogurt), cheese. Beans, lentils, tofu. Protein powder on the day normal food doesn't reach the number.\n\nIf you have kidney disease or any other medical condition, talk to your doctor before you change how much protein you eat. I'm a coach, not a doctor.\n\nThe full guide: {{link_article:how-much-protein}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "Pick the weekend meals on Friday morning", preheader: "Write down 1 or 2 meals before Friday night decides for you.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nMonday to Thursday runs on a plan. Friday night is the part nobody wrote down. By Sunday a lot of people have already decided to start again on Monday.\n\nWeekends are a common reason fat loss stalls. Saturday and Sunday often bring more food than weekdays. That alone can slow fat loss, or stall it.\n\nTwo things are easy to skip. Very strict food rules often end in overeating. And guilt around food tends to make losing weight harder than enjoying the meal and moving on.\n\nFriday morning, take 5 to 10 minutes. Pick 1 or 2 meals you actually want this weekend and write them down. Then when you're tired on Saturday night, there's already a plan, and a third big meal is less likely to just happen.\n\nAt the bigger meal, start with protein and vegetables. A solid portion is about 100 to 150 g of meat, fish or quark, plus vegetables. Protein is one of the most filling parts of a meal, so you're less likely to keep eating once the plate is empty.\n\nYou can tell a planned bigger meal by a few things: you chose it ahead of time, protein and vegetables are on the plate, and the next morning is a normal day. An unplanned one looks different. Nobody chose it, it goes on until you feel sick, and Monday becomes the restart.\n\nIf you went over, Monday is a normal day, without starving, detox or long cardio as a penalty. You can eat 100 to 200 kcal less for two or three days.\n\nIf you overeat again and again until you feel sick, you hide it, or you lose control around food and it scares you, talk to your doctor. That's not your fault, and it's something a doctor or specialist can really help with.\n\nMore on the weekend: {{link_article:weekend-overeating}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "A few drinks, and the extra food", preheader: "About 82 kcal more food after drinking, on average. A late meal can cost far more.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nBeer with dinner, a glass of wine, a shot on Friday. The weight can still go down.\n\nFat loss still comes down to the calorie deficit for the week. Reviews of the research mostly don't find a clear link between light or moderate drinking and weight gain. A glass with dinner a few times in the week is one situation. Six beers on Friday is a different one.\n\nAlcohol raises appetite, and you get less careful about the food in front of you. People eat more afterwards, and they don't eat less later to make up for it. A meta-analysis put that extra food at +343 kJ on average, about 82 kcal. That's an average across studies. On a real night out, the late pizza or kebab can easily cost more than the drinks did.\n\nTwo more things happen when you drink more, or more often:\n\nSleep gets worse. Alcohol suppresses REM sleep and breaks the night into pieces, mostly in the second half.\n\nMuscle building gets slower when the dose is high. In one study, muscle protein building after training was lower with alcohol, even with protein in the meal. The dose was about 12 drinks. That's a heavy night out.\n\nHealth is a separate question from the scale. In 2023 the WHO said that no amount of alcohol is safe for health. Alcohol is a Group 1 carcinogen, the group with the strongest evidence that it causes cancer in humans, and it's addictive. A calorie deficit doesn't cancel that.\n\nIf you drink, count the drinks in the day. A few drinks in the week, counted, aren't what stops fat loss.\n\nAll the studies are in the article: {{link_article:alcohol-and-fat-loss}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "Ab training for 6 weeks. The waist stayed.", preheader: "Their abs got fitter. Belly fat didn't change.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\n24 adults took part in a 6-week study. One group trained their abs 5 days a week, the other didn't, and everyone kept the same calories. The ab group did better on an endurance test. Abdominal fat didn't change in a significant way. The waist didn't either (Vispute, 2011).\n\nThe belly gets smaller when you lose fat across your whole body. For many people the belly is the last place to lose fat.\n\nA realistic pace is about 0.3 to 0.7 kg of fat a week.\n\n2 x 0.3 to 0.7 kg = 0.6 to 1.4 kg of fat in 14 days.\n\nOver two weeks, that usually starts to show in the weekly average. That's not a flat belly yet. The belly takes longer. A promise of a flat belly in 14 days is counting on water loss, and that water comes back after a few days.\n\nMeasure the waist once a week, the same way each time, next to the weekly average on the scale. In the ab study, calories stayed the same, and so did the waist.\n\nWhat helps the fat go: a mild deficit you can keep for months, protein around 1.6 to 2.2 g per kg of body weight, and lifting 2 to 4 times a week so the loss is fat and the muscle stays. Walking raises daily energy use without wearing you out. Short sleep and constant stress push hunger up and make the deficit harder to hold. The deficit still decides how much fat you lose.\n\nIf you suspect a hormone problem, or you take medication, talk to your doctor. Don't try to sort that out from an email. I'm a coach, not a doctor.\n\nMore: {{link_article:how-to-lose-belly-fat}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "Send me the menu before you go out", preheader: "A lighter beer, 0.5 l, is about 185 kcal. I'll mark your order in writing.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nBefore you leave for dinner, look up the kcal in one serving of the drink you'll actually order.\n\nA lighter beer (about 4% alcohol), 0.5 l, is about 185 kcal. A stronger one (about 5%), 0.5 l, is about 237 kcal. A 50 ml vodka shot is about 110 kcal. Dry white wine, 200 ml, is about 130 to 160 kcal, depending on the alcohol. Brands differ, so check yours. Sweet drinks add sugar on top of the alcohol.\n\nOn the night, stick to one kind of drink, and drink after you've eaten. If you want a record, take a photo of the plate and the glass. Write it down the next day from the photos. That's easier than opening calorie tables in front of friends.\n\nPart of a hangover is dehydration. Alcohol dries you out. Drink water through the evening, and put a full bottle next to the bed.\n\nIn the written coaching you send me the menu on WhatsApp, Monday to Friday: a business dinner, a hotel breakfast, a week of flights. I answer in writing, usually within 24 hours on working days, and I mark what to order. Dinners and trips go into your weekly plan, so they don't break it.\n\nAll of it happens in writing.\n\n{{price_block}}\n\nApply here: {{link_apply}}\n\nWant more support? Message me for VIP options.\n\nDrink numbers, in full: {{link_article:drinks-dinners-and-social-events}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "The scale is flat for two weeks", preheader: "Change one thing. About 10% less food, or 2,000 extra steps. Not both.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nTwo weeks on the same plan, and the weekly average on the scale hasn't moved.\n\nOne week is too short to judge. Water, food still in the gut, and for women the phase of the cycle can move the scale by two kilos inside that week. The swing says nothing about fat.\n\nWeigh every morning after the toilet, and compare weekly averages. If the average stays flat for two weeks in a row, and your log is accurate (weighed, oils and drinks included), change one thing:\n\ncut about 10% of calories, or add 2,000 steps a day.\n\nOne of the two. Not both in the same week.\n\nSkip the extra bans. Throwing out bread and cutting fruit won't help when the total intake doesn't fit.\n\nThe first four weeks, and this rule: {{link_article:how-to-start-losing-weight}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "Extra walking. Part of it came back.", preheader: "One small study. Food still decides most of it.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nIn one study, adults added a walking plan and burned extra energy every week. On average, the body took part of that back.\n\nIt was one small study (Knaan and colleagues, Communications Medicine, 2026). Use it as a reason to stop expecting steps to do the whole job.\n\nSeparate from that study: people often underreport what they eat, in some cases by 20 to 50%. Oil poured by eye. A piece of chocolate. A glass of wine. The rest of a child's plate. For your health, walk anyway. For fat loss, the food number has to be real.\n\nThat's the part I do with clients in writing. Every week you send the 3-minute report, and I recalculate calories and macros from it. The number moves with the week. You aren't stuck with the first guess from the calculator.\n\n{{price_block}}\n\nApply here: {{link_apply}}\n\nWant more support? Message me for VIP options.\n\nThe walking study: {{link_article:10000-steps-and-no-weight-loss}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "One hour of lifting in a bad week", preheader: "Two 30-minute sessions. From zero to some.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nSixty minutes in the whole week. That's two workouts of 30 minutes, or three of 20.\n\nA 2022 meta-analysis on strength training and risk of death (Shailendra and colleagues) linked any lifting, compared with none, to a lower risk of death from all causes.\n\nThis is observational. It shows a link, and it isn't proof that lifting causes a longer life. People who lift may also sleep better, smoke less or see a doctor more often. The papers are about risk of death, not about kilos of fat.\n\nWhen a week falls apart, this is the useful part: one hour fits. And in that research, the link was with any lifting compared with none.\n\nA simple hour:\n\nTwo full-body workouts, 30 minutes each. Four or five exercises: something for the legs (a squat or a lunge), a pull, a push, and one for the core. Two or three sets of 6 to 12 reps. The last two reps should feel heavy. You don't have to force the last possible rep. Write down the weight and the reps. When every set reaches the top of the range, add a little weight next time.\n\nAt home, body weight and a band are enough at the start. A gym helps later, when you need more load.\n\nIf you're pregnant, have had surgery, heart problems, or back pain that limits you, talk to your doctor or a physiotherapist first.\n\nMore: {{link_article:strength-training-one-hour-a-week}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "When the same menu stops working", preheader: "After a long diet the body adapts a little. Here's how we adjust.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nYou've held the same menu for a long time, and now the weight just sits there.\n\nAfter a long diet, the body does adapt. It's called adaptive thermogenesis. Resting energy use goes down a bit, and you move a bit less without noticing. The menu that used to work now holds your weight.\n\nThe size of that shift matters. It's smaller than it feels when the scale stops. The deficit still works. You may need to fine-tune the number. In most studies, the adaptation shrinks once people eat more again. It's an adjustment, and it can be undone.\n\nThat fine-tune is what the written check-in is for. You already know the weekly report from an earlier email. What changes here is what I do with it: when the week shows adaptation, I adjust the number based on how the week actually went, so you don't have to push harder on a menu that has stopped working.\n\nBetween reports, WhatsApp Monday to Friday. A menu, a hotel breakfast, a week that broke. I usually reply within 24 hours on working days. You write when it suits you. I'm in Czechia. Nothing needs us online at the same time.\n\nNo calls and no video calls. My English is better on paper than on a call, and I'd rather give you a precise answer than a fast one.\n\n{{price_block}}\n\nApply here: {{link_apply}}\n\nWant more support? Message me for VIP options.\n\nIf something feels seriously off (marked fatigue, feeling cold, hair loss, dry skin, swelling, a very low mood), don't guess, and don't read forums. See a doctor. If your mood is very low, talk to someone today: in Czechia you can call 116 123, elsewhere your local crisis line.\n\nMore on this: {{link_article:slow-metabolism-myth}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "Dinner at 10:30 pm is still just dinner", preheader: "A late dinner still fits. One trial found a gap, and what that gap means.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nThe work dinner is at 10:30 pm. You're in a hotel. Someone once told you that anything you eat after 6 pm goes straight to your waist.\n\nA bread roll at 10 pm isn't more fattening than the same roll at 8 am. Your body cares about energy in and energy out. Eat less than you burn over the week and you lose fat, whatever the clock says. There's no switch at 6 pm that stores the meal as fat.\n\nTiming still plays a part. In a trial of women on the same weight loss plan, the group eating an earlier dinner lost more weight than the group eating late in the evening. So timing counts, just far less than the day total. Part of the gap may be fewer evening snacks, though the trial can't say for sure.\n\nThe trip usually fails the other way. You eat almost nothing all day to pay for the late dinner, then the dinner and the snacks after it push the day over. Keep a real dinner, even a late one. Put protein and fiber on that plate so you aren't raiding the minibar an hour later.\n\nFit the hotel dinner into the day total. That's the number that counts.\n\nThe full article: {{link_article:eating-late-at-night}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "The fridge after a hard day", preheader: "Stress makes the deficit harder to hold. A few things make it easier.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nA long day, then the kitchen. The thing you want is sweet, and you want it now.\n\nChronic stress and short sleep can slow fat loss, and they can add to fat stored around the belly. Cortisol, the stress hormone, gets the blame, and people call the result a hormonal belly. But there's a limit. Hormones don't build a belly on their own against a calorie deficit. They make the deficit harder to hold. Sleep and stress change how easily you stay under your number, and how much you eat. They don't get around the number.\n\nA very strict diet does the same from the other side. Exhausted and hungry, you're pushed to eat more and move less. Sweet food is the fastest energy, so that's what you crave. A meal with beans lands slower: protein and fiber with the carbs, steadier energy.\n\nFor a week like that:\n\nKeep a regular time to sleep and to get up. Fewer screens in the evening. A walk still counts.\n\nPut protein and fiber in the meal before the hour when the cravings usually show up.\n\nIf you're very tired and very hungry, a diet break can help. That means a planned pause from dieting, not a blow-up and a restart on Monday.\n\nIf stress feels like more than a hard week, or eating feels out of your control, talk to your doctor or a mental health professional. In the Czech Republic, you can call the psychological first aid line on 116 123.\n\nIf you suspect a hormone problem, or you take medication, talk to your doctor. I'm a coach, not a doctor.\n\nStress and the belly: {{link_article:how-to-lose-belly-fat}}\nHunger and cravings: {{link_article:cravings-and-hunger}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "Lifting: 21% lower risk of death. Add cardio: 40%.", preheader: "A 2019 review, two numbers. I write the hour into your real week.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nA 2019 review pooled studies on strength training and the risk of death (Saeidifard and colleagues).\n\nStrength training on its own was linked to a 21% lower risk of death from all causes. Strength training plus aerobic activity was linked to a 40% lower risk. The papers measured aerobic activity in general. Walking is the easiest kind to fit into a busy week, so that's what I'd keep.\n\nThis is observational. It shows a link. It doesn't prove the gym caused the difference. Another review came out with different numbers, so I wouldn't treat 21 or 40 as an exact promise. I go by the direction: weights and walking add up. And the lifting doesn't have to be five days in the gym.\n\nThese papers count deaths. Your waist is a separate question. Still, they're the reason a crowded week doesn't need a new sport. About one hour of lifting, plus the steps you already collect. An earlier email covered how to split that hour when the week breaks. This one is about keeping both: the hour and the walking.\n\nIf you want that hour written onto the days you actually have, that's the coaching. I set the training for your real schedule, in writing. Once a week you send a 3-minute report from your phone, training included. I answer in writing with the next week.\n\nNo calls and no video calls.\n\n{{price_block}}\n\nApply here: {{link_apply}}\n\nWant more support? Message me for VIP options.\n\nThe review: {{link_article:strength-training-one-hour-a-week}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "8.5 hours vs 5.5. Same 3 kg, different split.", preheader: "10 people, 14 days in a lab. Short sleep took more lean mass than fat.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nSame diet, 14 days, 10 people in a lab. The scale dropped about 3 kg both times. The split didn't.\n\nWith 8.5 hours in bed they lost 1.4 kg of fat and 1.5 kg of lean mass. With 5.5 hours in bed they lost 0.6 kg of fat and 2.4 kg of lean mass. Short sleep, and most of what left wasn't fat. It was a small study. Everything in it was controlled.\n\nOther work points the same way. People with overweight who slept longer ate less. One night without sleep lowered how much muscle protein the body built. In another diet study where people cut back on sleep on several nights a week, less of the weight they lost came from fat.\n\nOutside a lab you also move less when you're tired, and the food log usually runs low. The gap is probably bigger.\n\nI put sleep in the top four, with the deficit, protein, and strength training. One bad night won't wreck the diet. If you already sleep 7 to 9 hours most nights, work on something else. Very few people do well on very little sleep. A few families carry a gene for natural short sleep. It's rare enough that you're very unlikely to be one of them.\n\nThe study: {{link_article:sleep-and-fat-loss}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "10,000 steps was a pedometer slogan", preheader: "Add 2,000 to 3,000 to your own average. Most of the benefit shows up earlier.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nThe number 10,000 didn't come from a fat loss study. In Japan it was a marketing line for a pedometer. The device was called manpo-kei, a \"10,000 steps meter\". A round number that sold.\n\nWhat the health research shows is earlier than that. In a pooled analysis of 15 studies, the drop in the risk of dying leveled off around 6,000 to 8,000 steps for people aged 60 and over, and around 8,000 to 10,000 for younger adults (Paluch, 2022). In older women, about 4,400 steps a day already meant a lower risk than about 2,700 (Lee, 2019). Both papers measured the risk of dying. Fat loss wasn't part of them. Miss 10,000 and you haven't missed the point.\n\nFor fat loss, start from your own average. Add 2,000 to 3,000 steps. If you walk 4,000 now, 6,000 to 7,000 is the next step. From there, build toward 8,000. A long-term guide for most people is 7,000 to 10,000 a day. Use it as a guide.\n\n7,000 steps every day for a year beats 20,000 once a week and a chair the other days.\n\nYou don't need a free hour. Take a call while you walk. Get off one stop early. Use the stairs. The steps sit in the gaps you already have.\n\nOne workout is one hour out of twenty-four. The rest of the day's movement, walking included, often burns more than that hour. Some people come out of a hard session starving. A walk rarely does that, so it's easier not to eat it back.\n\nFood still sets most of the deficit. Walking is the easy part to keep doing.\n\nMore: {{link_article:how-many-steps-a-day}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "2,500 kcal extra = 5 days of deficit gone", preheader: "On a trip, one meal can be the exception. Not every dinner.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\n7 days at -500 kcal = -3,500 kcal in the bank.\n\nThen a day with no brakes. 2,000 to 3,000 kcal extra is easy. Take the middle:\n\n2,500 / 500 = 5\n\nFive days of the deficit, gone in one day.\n\nA work trip is where this happens without anyone calling it a cheat day. Breakfast at the hotel, lunch with the team, dinner with the client, and a drink after. Four exceptions in one day is a day off the plan. On a five-day trip, that's five of them.\n\nOne relaxed meal fits. A whole day doesn't. Pick the one meal before you fly. Pizza, the tasting menu, whatever you actually want. The next meal is a normal meal. One dinner isn't a free pass for the rest of the city.\n\nIf the foods you like are already in the normal week, you don't need a special day at all.\n\nIn the written coaching I set that before the trip: which meal is the one, and what the other meals look like. You aren't deciding it in the taxi. It's in the week, in writing.\n\nNo calls and no video calls.\n\n{{price_block}}\n\nApply here: {{link_apply}}\n\nWant more support? Message me for VIP options.\n\nThe math: {{link_article:cheat-day-or-cheat-meal}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "Protein per 100 kcal, from the label", preheader: "Same column, two lines. 20 g and 100 kcal isn't 10 g and 200 kcal.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nThe front of the pack can say high protein. The table is the part that tells you if that's true for the calories.\n\nRead protein and energy from the same column. Then the serving size drops out of the comparison.\n\nProtein per 100 kcal = protein grams / kcal x 100.\n\nIf that column says 20 g of protein and 100 kcal:\n\n20 / 100 x 100 = 20 g of protein per 100 kcal.\n\nIf it says 10 g of protein and 200 kcal:\n\n10 / 200 x 100 = 5 g per 100 kcal.\n\nThe first line gives you 4 times as much protein for the calories. Those two lines only show the arithmetic. Use the numbers printed on the pack in front of you.\n\nThat's the same ranking as the protein tool. It sorts common foods by protein per 100 kcal, so you can see which foods get you to your protein target without eating up your calorie budget. You can also type the price and the pack size from your own shop and see what 100 g of protein costs you.\n\nhttps://martinbarna.cz/en/protein/\n\nYou already have your daily protein target. The label tells you which foods get you there cheapest in calories.\n\nThe article: {{link_article:how-much-protein}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "Willpower is rarely why the weight comes back", preheader: "After a very low calorie diet, appetite hormones were still off a year later. Change the setup.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\n\"I'll be strict for a month, then eat normally again.\"\n\nLook at the word normally. If normally means the way of eating that put the weight on, you get the same result. A diet with an end date runs out. What lasts is a change you'd still keep a year from now.\n\nYo-yo dieting usually comes from a bad setup. Your character has little to do with it. Three things show up together: a deficit that's too big, muscle lost along the way, and a plan that was always going to stop.\n\nLess muscle lowers what you burn at rest a little. When the old portions come back, there's less room for them.\n\nThere's a second push, and it isn't in your head. Sumithran and colleagues (2011) put 50 people on a very low calorie diet. A year later, in those who finished, appetite hormones still hadn't returned to pre-diet levels. Hunger stays louder. Willpower doesn't do much against that.\n\nA different study followed 14 people from an extreme weight-loss competition for six years. Their resting metabolism was still about 700 kcal a day below where it started, roughly 500 kcal more than their new body composition and age would explain. That was a TV competition with extreme training and dieting. It says little about your mild deficit. There, the picture I see is different: you want the old portions, and the hunger lasts longer than you hoped. The harder you cut, the harder the body defends the old weight.\n\nThe setup I see hold up best:\n\n- a mild deficit, 15 to 20% below your energy use\n- protein around 1.6 to 2 g per kg\n- lifting, so the body has a reason to keep muscle\n- after the diet, add calories back to maintenance step by step, and stay there for a while\n\nWhat keeps the weight off is a way of eating you can live with, long after the month is over.\n\nMore: {{link_article:yo-yo-dieting}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "A flat scale at 45 isn't a dead metabolism", preheader: "Per kg of fat-free mass, energy use holds steady from 20 to 60. What changes is how much you move.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nThe weekly average has sat still, and the first story is age. Forty, forty-five, fifty. The metabolism must have switched off.\n\nA large doubly labeled water dataset says otherwise. It covers daily energy use in people from 8 days old to 95 years (Pontzer, 2021). Per kg of fat-free mass (muscle, organs, bone, water), energy use stays stable from 20 to 60. It starts to fall only later. Age 40 isn't a break point in that data.\n\nWhat does change is the muscle itself. An MRI study of 468 men and women, aged 18 to 88, found the share of muscle in body weight falling from the 20s. A clear drop in the amount of muscle showed up toward the late 40s, mostly in the lower half of the body (Janssen, 2000). Less muscle, less energy use. The cause is often how the day changed: fewer stairs, shorter walks, more time in the car and at the desk.\n\nSo a flat month isn't a reason to crash the food to 1,200 kcal. After 40 that trade is worse than at 20. A crash diet tends to cost more muscle, and rebuilding it gets slower with age.\n\nAn earlier email already has the small move when two honest weeks are flat. This one is why you don't throw the mild plan out because a birthday arrived. Keep the muscle and keep walking. The two-week check is for small tweaks. Whether the plan works shows over months.\n\nMore: {{link_article:fat-loss-after-40}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "Protein and lifting worked together", preheader: "In a 10-week study, only the group doing both significantly gained fat-free mass while losing weight.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nA Dutch study put 100 people, aged 55 to 80, on a calorie deficit for ten weeks. Four groups: more protein or not, weight training three times a week or not (Verreijen, 2017).\n\nIn the main analysis, protein alone didn't change fat-free mass in a clear way. Training alone didn't either. The only group whose fat-free mass went up significantly during the loss did both, by 0.6 kg (p = 0.011).\n\nRead the small print before you quote 0.6 kg at anyone. Everyone in the study was 55 to 80, older than many people reading this. The result sat inside one group. It wasn't the headline effect of the whole study. The direction is what I take from it. The two levers work together. Each one on its own pulls less.\n\nThat's how the coaching plan is built. After payment you fill in an intake form. Within 48 hours you have your calories, protein and training plan, all at once. The minimum version for a messy week is agreed before that week arrives. Each week, one short guide based on your last check-in.\n\nNo calls and no video calls. You get the plan and the numbers in writing.\n\nIf you take medication that affects weight, talk to your doctor first. I'm a coach, not a doctor.\n\n{{price_block}}\n\nApply here: {{link_apply}}\n\nWant more support? Message me for VIP options.\n\nThe study: {{link_article:fat-loss-after-40}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "A work dinner you didn't choose", preheader: "Alcohol is 7 kcal per gram. Pick the drink before the first toast.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nSomeone else booked the restaurant. The toast is already on the agenda. You can still lose fat. You need the decision before you sit down.\n\nAlcohol has 7 kcal per gram. Protein and carbs have 4 kcal per gram. Fat has 9. The stronger the drink, the more alcohol per sip, and the more energy. Sweet mixers add their own. Your body doesn't need the alcohol. It's still energy, same as the rest of the day.\n\nBefore the dinner, write one line for the drink you'll actually order: the name, the pour, the kcal. Use that line again at the next event. Brands differ, so check yours once, not from memory at the table.\n\nOn the night, stick to that one kind. Drink it after you've eaten. If you drink, write it down. A couple of lighter drinks, counted, is a different evening from one that ends drunk.\n\nIf you trained that day, a drunk finish is the expensive version. Your body burns the alcohol first, and heavy drinking after training slows muscle repair. Stay well below drunk and the load is much smaller.\n\nYou don't have to drink at all. If you do, count it, and the week can still trend down.\n\nThe numbers and the rules: {{link_article:drinks-dinners-and-social-events}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "The minimum when the hotel has no gym", preheader: "Log the meal when you eat it. Walking 3,000 steps? Aim for 6,000, not 12,000.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nNo gym this week. The hotel one is closed, or you already know you won't use it. Fat loss can keep going without it. You lose a lever, so the food has to be more accurate, and you pay for the missing training somewhere else.\n\nLog the meal when you eat it, not at midnight from memory. The misses are the oil, the bites, the drink you didn't write down. Looking back is where the day goes soft.\n\nProtein can cover a little of what the gym does. A meta-analysis of 24 studies and 1,063 people compared low-fat diets with the same calories but different amounts of protein (Wycherley, 2012). Higher protein meant 0.79 kg more weight loss and 0.87 kg more fat loss. It softened the loss of fat-free mass by 0.43 kg, and it softened the drop in resting energy use by 595 kJ a day, about 142 kcal. The authors call that modest. So do I. Think of it as insurance, then put a protein food in the meals you can actually get.\n\nSteps: start from where you are. If you walk 3,000 now, 6,000 is a better first target than a fantasy 12,000. The 8,000 from the kit can come after. Stairs and the walk between meetings count. You don't need a sports session to change that line.\n\nOne warning. The study behind the big diet-only results ran people on a very low energy diet. Leave that one in the paper.\n\nWhen the week is normal again, add the lifting back. Until then, food and steps still move the result.\n\nMore: {{link_article:lose-weight-without-exercise}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "The diet cola at the airport is fine", preheader: "Swapping regular cola for diet cuts the calories. The gut scare is shaky in people.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nAirport lounge, long afternoon, you want something cold. You might reach for the regular cola because you've heard the diet one is worse. That it ruins your gut, that it makes you crave sweets.\n\nThe studies in people say otherwise.\n\nWhen diet sodas replace sugary drinks, people lose a little weight and their metabolic markers improve. Switching to water points the same way, though the data on it are thinner. The loss comes from the sugar you stopped drinking. The sweetener itself doesn't burn fat.\n\nIn humans, the gut story is shaky too. A small randomized, double-blind crossover trial on aspartame and sucralose found minimal effect on the gut microbiome. A 2023 review found mixed results in people: some trials saw changes, but many randomized trials found no significant effect.\n\nOn the road, when the choice is regular or diet, take the diet one. Water is a good choice too. The sugary drink is where calories leak, because you don't even notice you drank them.\n\nIt works best when the rest of your day has enough protein and vegetables. A diet cola won't rescue a day of pastries. And if you have a diagnosis, ask your doctor.\n\nThe full article: {{link_article:diet-soda-and-weight-loss}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "About 14 g of fiber per 1,000 kcal", preheader: "Almost no calories, and you stay full longer. Add it slowly.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nLook at a typical business lunch: meat, white rice or fries, a sauce. The protein is there. The fiber isn't, and that's one reason you're hungry again so soon.\n\nFiber is the part of plant food your body can't digest. It brings almost no calories and it still does a lot. It fills you up, so you go longer without hunger and pick at food less. Some types also slow down how fast sugar from the meal gets absorbed.\n\nGuidelines land around 25 to 38 g a day, depending on how much you eat. Easy rule: about 14 g per 1,000 kcal. On 2,000 kcal a day that's about 28 g.\n\nWhere to find it on a menu or a hotel buffet:\n\n- Vegetables, as a side or a salad, with every meal you can.\n- Pulses: lentils, beans, chickpeas.\n- Oats and whole grains.\n- Fruit, with the skin where that makes sense.\n- Nuts and seeds.\n\nYou don't have to eat like a rabbit. A portion of vegetables at each meal and pulses a few times a week is enough to start.\n\nGo slowly, though. Going from almost nothing to a lot overnight can leave you bloated for the first few days. Add it step by step and drink enough water.\n\nThe full article: {{link_article:fiber-and-fat-loss}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "Fewer sets or more on a hard diet", preheader: "Both groups kept their lean mass. More sets added more strength. It's still a preprint.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nIt's tempting to cut your training when a diet starts. Less food, worse recovery, so less work. Sounds logical.\n\nA recent study pushes back on that. Advanced lifters ate in a large deficit, with plenty of protein, for a few weeks. Half trained a moderate number of sets per muscle each week. The other half trained far more.\n\nOn DXA scans there was no statistically significant difference in body composition. Both groups lost fat and kept their lean mass. Strength is where they differed. The high-volume group gained more on the strength tests.\n\nNow the small print. It's a preprint, still under review. In a few weeks, most of the strength gain is likely the nervous system getting better at the lifts. And DXA can't fully separate muscle from water and glycogen. A deficit that deep is aggressive, and these people already lifted. For me it's a signal worth watching. I wouldn't build a rule on it yet.\n\nWhat I take from it: don't cut sets on a diet just out of habit. Let the plan decide that.\n\nThat's what the written training plan in coaching is for. I build it around your real schedule. Once a week you send me a 3-minute report from your phone, training included. I answer in writing with the plan for the next week.\n\nNo calls and no video calls.\n\n{{price_block}}\n\nApply here: {{link_apply}}\n\nWant more support? Message me for VIP options.\n\nThe full article: {{link_article:training-volume-while-dieting}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "Protein after the gym: your daily total matters more", preheader: "Strength climbs up to about 1.5 g per kg. Above that, the curve flattens.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nA late session in the hotel gym, then a meeting. People ask me if the shake has to land the minute the last set ends. What matters more is how much protein you eat across the whole day.\n\nOn paper, two sources look like they disagree. Morton and colleagues (2018) found muscle gains level off around 1.6 g per kg of body weight on average. Refalo and Helms (2025) give a higher range. Their range is per kg of fat-free mass, though, not of what the scale shows. Convert it to body weight for a typical lifter and their lower edge lands close to the same place. At the low end the two meet. The top end is still debated.\n\nOn strength, Tagawa (2022) shows the curve climbing up to about 1.5 g per kg and flattening above that. Past that point, extra protein buys very little extra strength.\n\nWhat I use with clients: 1.6 to 2.2 g per kg of total body weight. I go toward 2.2 as insurance when the food log is rough (the error is often 20 to 50%) or the fat-loss phase is hard.\n\nAnd protein isn't the first lever. Calories, training and sleep come first. Without those, extra protein mostly costs money.\n\nOn a trip, don't chase the shake. Get protein into whatever meals the day gives you.\n\nThe full article: {{link_article:protein-timing-after-workout}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "A planned week at maintenance isn't quitting", preheader: "The early data on diet breaks looked great, later studies less so. A planned pause still has its uses.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nSome weeks the diet just doesn't fit. I usually hear about two options then: push through, or drop everything. There's a third one: a planned break at maintenance.\n\nOne study in men with obesity compared two groups. Group 1 dieted for 16 weeks at 67% of their usual calories. Group 2 also dieted for 16 weeks, but after every two weeks of diet they ate at maintenance for two weeks, so their whole diet took 30 weeks. Group 2 lost noticeably more fat.\n\nLater research didn't back that up. A review of nine trials found planned pauses no more or less effective than steady dieting for weight loss. So I don't sell a break as a fat-loss trick.\n\nIt still earns its place. A hard week, a party, a stretch where you need rest and don't want to gain. Eat at your current maintenance and you shouldn't regain fat. You just pause the loss. The scale may still go up a kilo or two from water and glycogen. That isn't fat, and it usually comes off again once the diet resumes.\n\nKeep in mind that maintenance drops as your weight drops. If you held your weight on 2,000 kcal at the start, a few weeks later that number is lower. The break week isn't the menu from before the diet.\n\nChanging your body takes months. You don't have to lose weight every single week.\n\nThe full article: {{link_article:diet-breaks}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "Fat loss on night shifts: what still works", preheader: "Night shifts come with about 23% higher odds of overweight. The physics still holds.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nThis one is for you if you work nights or a rotating roster. If that's not you, feel free to skip it.\n\nShift work is clearly linked to weight gain. A 2018 meta-analysis put the odds of overweight or obesity about 23% higher with night shift work. A 2021 meta-analysis found shift work significantly linked to metabolic syndrome. This isn't a willpower problem.\n\nWhat happens: a disrupted body clock throws off hunger and fullness signals. Your body isn't sure when it has had enough. Add worse sleep, a pull toward something fast, and whatever food is on offer at 3 a.m.\n\nThe physics still holds on a night shift. Calories don't disappear. What shifts break is your ability to stick with the plan. So build the plan for days that don't go by the book.\n\n- Put the main meal before the shift or after it, not in the middle of the night.\n- Bring food from home, so 3 a.m. doesn't choose for you.\n- Make sleep the priority. Don't answer a bad week by cutting calories harder. Less sleep means more hunger.\n- If your workplace allows it, a planned 20- to 30-minute nap mid-shift can help alertness.\n\nThe full article: {{link_article:shift-work-and-fat-loss}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "Decide the default before you're hungry", preheader: "Meals decided in the morning beat whatever's closest at 6 pm.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nOn a busy day, food decisions happen at the worst moment. You're hungry, you're late, and whatever is closest wins.\n\nPlanning moves the decision earlier. When the meals are decided ahead of time, you know what the day holds, and the random snack you grab in a rush is easier to skip. Same on a trip: decide the default before you're hungry.\n\nThe deficit doesn't need to be dramatic. 300 to 500 kcal below what your day burns is a deficit you can actually keep. A steady rhythm helps, for example 3 main meals and 1 or 2 snacks. Meal frequency doesn't change your metabolism, so pick the number that fits your calendar.\n\nThe part people skip is the edit. A plan you never touch goes stale. One you revisit each week holds up much better.\n\nThat weekly edit is the core of written coaching. Once a week you send me a 3-minute report from your phone. I answer in writing with the plan for next week, based on how last week actually went. For a messy week, we agree on a minimum plan in advance, so you're not improvising halfway through it.\n\nIt all happens in writing, on your schedule.\n\n{{price_block}}\n\nApply here: {{link_apply}}\n\nWant more support? Message me for VIP options.\n\nThe full article: {{link_article:meal-planning-for-fat-loss}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "The hotel breakfast is optional", preheader: "A 2014 trial and a 2019 BMJ meta-analysis found breakfast didn't help weight loss.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nThe hotel breakfast is included and the buffet is open all morning. It's easy to eat it just because it's paid for, and because breakfast is \"the most important meal of the day\".\n\nThat line was popularized by food marketing about a century ago.\n\nThe trials: in 2014, Emily Dhurandhar and colleagues randomly told people trying to lose weight to eat breakfast or skip it. No discernible effect on weight loss. In 2019, a BMJ meta-analysis of randomized trials found that people who added breakfast tended to eat more across the day, about 260 kcal on average. Breakfast wasn't a reliable weight loss tool.\n\nSkipping it won't slow your metabolism either. The energy you spend digesting food follows how much you eat across the day, whatever time you start.\n\nOn a trip, ask yourself one thing: does breakfast help you stay on plan, or does it get in the way?\n\n- Eat it if you're hungry in the morning, if you train early, or if skipping it makes the evening hunger hit hard.\n- Feel free to skip it if you're not hungry and you only eat it because it's there.\n\nIf you take medication for blood sugar or have a history of disordered eating, check with your doctor before changing when you eat.\n\nOne rule either way. Don't skip breakfast \"for fat loss\" and then make up for it at night. If that happens to you, eat the breakfast, with protein in it.\n\nThe full article: {{link_article:do-you-need-breakfast-to-lose-weight}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "Low-carb or low-fat? About 26 kcal a day apart.", preheader: "Set calories and protein. Split carbs and fat by taste and training.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nSome people weigh every gram of carbs and fat, sure that the ratio decides their fat loss. Once calories and protein are set, the ratio barely moves the result.\n\nHall and Guo pooled 32 controlled feeding studies that swapped carbs for fat at equal calories. The lower-fat diets came out slightly ahead: people burned about 26 kcal a day more. That's a bite or two of food.\n\nSacks followed 811 people for about two years on four different ratios. They lost about the same amount of weight.\n\nProtein is a different story. In a trial by Leidy and colleagues, at the same weight loss, the higher-protein group lost noticeably less lean mass.\n\nWhat's worth tracking: calories, protein, and fiber for fullness. Split carbs and fat by taste and by training. That's good news on the road. Pasta and steak can both fit, as long as your daily total and protein stay on track.\n\nTwo caveats. Keep fat at roughly 25 to 35% of calories and don't stay below 20% for long. And if you train hard, eat enough carbs to fuel it.\n\nThe full article: {{link_article:macros-and-fat-loss}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "48 kcal a minute vs 31", preheader: "Same calories on offer. People ate 508 kcal a day more on the ultra-processed menu.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nA bag of pretzel sticks in the hotel room is gone in ten minutes. A plate of potatoes and chicken isn't. There's a measured reason for that.\n\nKevin Hall kept 20 adults on a ward for four weeks (2019). Two weeks on ultra-processed food, two weeks on unprocessed food. Both menus offered matched calories, macros, sugar, salt and fiber, and people could eat as much as they wanted. On the ultra-processed menu they ate 508 kcal a day more and gained 0.9 kg in those two weeks. On the unprocessed menu they lost 0.9 kg.\n\nThe physics worked as usual. People gained because they ate more, and the food let them eat more without noticing.\n\nOne likely lever is speed. Ciarán Forde and colleagues later highlighted the eating rate measured in that trial. Ultra-processed: 48 kcal a minute. Unprocessed: 31. Fullness takes a while to catch up with what you've eaten. Eat the same energy in about two-thirds of the time, and \"I've had enough\" shows up when you're already well ahead.\n\nTexture seems to matter as much as the label. In another trial, harder versions of the same lunches were eaten more slowly, and people took in 26% less energy from them.\n\nWhat you can do with any menu or minibar, without banning anything:\n\n- Pick the harder, bulkier version of the same food. Boiled potatoes instead of mash. Whole nuts instead of a nut bar.\n- Put the soft, dense food on a plate instead of eating it from the bag.\n\nThe full article: {{link_article:ultra-processed-food}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "One tablespoon of oil, about ten sugar cubes", preheader: "Your body counts the calories in the granola, whatever the label says.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nOrganic, raw, \"guilt-free\", superfood. Labels like that make people feel they can eat as much as they want. Your body counts the calories either way.\n\nSome \"healthy\" foods are dense:\n\n- Nuts and nut butters. A good food, and a handful is fine. Half a jar of peanut butter is close to 1,000 kcal.\n- Oil, including the best olive oil. A tablespoon of oil has about as many calories as ten sugar cubes.\n- Avocado. Half at breakfast is fine. Three a day will show up.\n- Granola, muesli bars, \"guilt-free\" desserts. They often have as much sugar and fat as a regular sweet, with a nicer wrapper and a higher price.\n\nIt works the other way too. A sane amount of chocolate won't make you gain. No food is clean or dirty on its own. Amount and context decide.\n\nThis is how a careful eater can stall: the food is good, the amounts aren't counted. The weekly report in written coaching shows it. You fill it in on your phone once a week, in about 3 minutes. I look at the total for the week and answer in writing with the plan for next week, adjusting calories and macros based on how your week actually went.\n\nEverything stays in writing, on your schedule.\n\n{{price_block}}\n\nApply here: {{link_apply}}\n\nWant more support? Message me for VIP options.\n\nThe full article: {{link_article:clean-eating-myth}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" },
  { subject: "Eat the fruit. Count the airport smoothie.", preheader: "Two or three portions of whole fruit a day fit a diet. Juice and dried fruit are where it leaks.", body: "Hi {{first_name}},\n\n\"A banana is all sugar.\" \"Fruit makes you fat, it has fructose.\" I hear this a lot, and people end up throwing out one of the best foods they could eat.\n\nFruit contains sugar. Fat gain comes from eating more energy than you spend, for long enough, wherever that energy comes from.\n\nWhole fruit is mostly water and fiber. A big volume brings few calories. An apple or a bowl of berries fills you up before you could overshoot. The fructose worry is about large amounts of liquid, concentrated sugar. A liter of soda, not an apple or a handful of raspberries.\n\nFruit gets away from you in two forms:\n\n- Juice and smoothies. Pressing strips out most of the fiber. Blending keeps it, but you skip the chewing, and that's a big part of what made the fruit filling. A few pieces you'd chew for a while go down in half a minute.\n- Dried fruit. The water is gone, so a small handful holds a lot of calories and sugar, and fills you less.\n\nAt the hotel, take from the fruit bowl at reception. The big airport smoothie is a drink with calories, so count it like one.\n\nWhat I recommend to clients: two or three portions of whole fruit a day, counted inside the day's calories. One banana won't wreck a diet.\n\nThe full article: {{link_article:fruit-and-weight-loss}}\n\nBe Effective!\nMartin" }
];
